WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.339
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:07.650
ANDI GUTMANS: Hi, I'm Andi
Gutmans, General Manager

00:00:07.650 --> 00:00:11.040
and VP of Engineering for
Databases for Google Cloud.

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:13.620
Thank you for joining
us for Google I/O.

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:16.133
We have an exciting product
announcement to share with you

00:00:16.133 --> 00:00:16.800
in this session.

00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:21.920
For developers and
enterprises alike, the cloud

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:25.100
offers organizations
agility, cost savings,

00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:27.410
and differentiated
capabilities that

00:00:27.410 --> 00:00:30.600
allows developers to do
more than they could before.

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:34.700
That's why 75% of all databases
are expected to be in the cloud

00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:35.780
this year.

00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:40.670
Imagine, 40 years of proprietary
databases placed on premises

00:00:40.670 --> 00:00:42.920
are moving so
rapidly to the cloud.

00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:44.900
It's amazing.

00:00:44.900 --> 00:00:46.400
New applications
are already being

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:49.790
built in a cloud-first manner,
and existing applications

00:00:49.790 --> 00:00:54.010
are moving to the
cloud at a rapid pace.

00:00:54.010 --> 00:00:55.600
Today, those
customers already have

00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:57.280
many options on Google Cloud.

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:00.070
We offer a full suite
of database services,

00:01:00.070 --> 00:01:03.310
from in-memory services,
like Memorystore for Redis,

00:01:03.310 --> 00:01:06.670
to relational database services,
like Cloud SQL and Spanner,

00:01:06.670 --> 00:01:08.800
to nonrelational
database services,

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:11.380
like Firestore and Bigtable.

00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:14.110
All these services help
manage the complexity

00:01:14.110 --> 00:01:16.240
of running your
databases so that they

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:19.930
are secure, simple to
use, and operate, and make

00:01:19.930 --> 00:01:23.590
app development faster
and easier for you.

00:01:23.590 --> 00:01:25.930
And we're really excited about
what these databases have

00:01:25.930 --> 00:01:28.960
accomplished both in terms
of their ability to scale

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:31.720
and the number of customer
workloads we've served.

00:01:31.720 --> 00:01:34.330
To give you a sense for the
scale of which our databases

00:01:34.330 --> 00:01:38.410
operate, Spanner processes
over 2 billion requests

00:01:38.410 --> 00:01:39.790
per second at peak.

00:01:39.790 --> 00:01:41.680
Isn't that amazing?

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:44.440
Bigtable, our fully
managed key-value database,

00:01:44.440 --> 00:01:48.940
processes over 5 billion
requests per second at peak.

00:01:48.940 --> 00:01:51.790
Firestore is our serverless
mobile-optimized document

00:01:51.790 --> 00:01:54.100
database, which
powers applications

00:01:54.100 --> 00:01:59.870
with more than 750 million
monthly active end users.

00:01:59.870 --> 00:02:02.590
So this demonstrates the
scale of the workloads

00:02:02.590 --> 00:02:04.940
that our databases support.

00:02:04.940 --> 00:02:07.880
Our databases help our
customers modernize their apps

00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:12.200
and unlock the value of their
data across the GCP ecosystem.

00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:17.150
For example, more than 650,000
Google Kubernetes Engine pods

00:02:17.150 --> 00:02:19.490
securely connect to Cloud SQL.

00:02:19.490 --> 00:02:23.690
And BigQuery users
query over 125 petabytes

00:02:23.690 --> 00:02:28.590
of data in Cloud SQL
on average in a month.

00:02:28.590 --> 00:02:30.720
That said, we still
have more work

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:33.450
to do to support customers
moving to the cloud,

00:02:33.450 --> 00:02:36.180
especially for customers
coming from legacy,

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:39.000
proprietary databases.

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:40.830
These customers are
not moving to the cloud

00:02:40.830 --> 00:02:42.810
with their applications as is.

00:02:42.810 --> 00:02:46.260
They're working hard to
modernize by freeing themselves

00:02:46.260 --> 00:02:50.250
of expensive licenses and
hostile business practices,

00:02:50.250 --> 00:02:53.730
adapting to new requirements
around vendor portability

00:02:53.730 --> 00:02:55.710
and taking advantage
of innovations

00:02:55.710 --> 00:02:59.200
that only the cloud can deliver.

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:01.570
Those customers are
increasingly standardizing

00:03:01.570 --> 00:03:04.000
on open source,
and in particular,

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:06.250
one open source
database, Postgres.

00:03:09.170 --> 00:03:10.770
And there's a reason for that.

00:03:10.770 --> 00:03:13.280
It has everything you would
expect from an open source

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:14.390
database--

00:03:14.390 --> 00:03:18.380
the portability that has become
so important to enterprises,

00:03:18.380 --> 00:03:20.210
and a license-free
cost model that

00:03:20.210 --> 00:03:23.130
enables companies of any size.

00:03:23.130 --> 00:03:24.950
But what's so special
about Postgres

00:03:24.950 --> 00:03:27.770
is its rich functionality
and extensibility.

00:03:27.770 --> 00:03:30.560
It provides a similar
and, in some cases,

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:33.680
better set of functionality
than the expensive closed source

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:35.090
alternatives.

00:03:35.090 --> 00:03:38.510
It supports parallel
query, partition tables,

00:03:38.510 --> 00:03:42.860
stored procedures, replication,
change data capture,

00:03:42.860 --> 00:03:45.260
rich data models, and more.

00:03:45.260 --> 00:03:47.510
Postgres's ecosystem
of extensions,

00:03:47.510 --> 00:03:50.720
from foreign data wrappers
to geospatial support,

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:54.450
helps it address a wide
variety of use cases.

00:03:54.450 --> 00:03:56.150
It's also ubiquitous.

00:03:56.150 --> 00:03:58.790
It plugs into every major
application development

00:03:58.790 --> 00:04:00.530
framework or ecosystem.

00:04:00.530 --> 00:04:04.160
It's adopted by millions
of users in every industry,

00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:06.950
from startups to
large enterprises,

00:04:06.950 --> 00:04:10.490
and has demonstrated itself in
demanding customer environments

00:04:10.490 --> 00:04:14.180
time and time again, giving
others the confidence to use it

00:04:14.180 --> 00:04:16.420
in their own applications.

00:04:16.420 --> 00:04:18.760
And the community is
friendly and welcoming

00:04:18.760 --> 00:04:20.979
with lots of support for
those getting started.

00:04:23.540 --> 00:04:26.000
We've worked with lots
of customers in Postgres,

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:28.310
running every scale
of application,

00:04:28.310 --> 00:04:32.910
from early development to true
enterprise-grade applications.

00:04:32.910 --> 00:04:35.450
And what we found is that,
despite the benefits,

00:04:35.450 --> 00:04:37.670
at times, there's
too much burden

00:04:37.670 --> 00:04:40.920
on developers to make it
run well in production.

00:04:40.920 --> 00:04:43.520
We see developers struggle
to tune parameters

00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:46.610
and configure their setup,
make difficult trade-offs

00:04:46.610 --> 00:04:50.240
between reliability,
cost, and performance.

00:04:50.240 --> 00:04:53.450
They're using layers of proxies
to get high availability,

00:04:53.450 --> 00:04:56.660
carefully managing storage and
memory resources to prevent

00:04:56.660 --> 00:04:59.420
out-of-disk and
out-of-memory errors,

00:04:59.420 --> 00:05:03.230
and navigating the impact
of Postgres VACUUM.

00:05:03.230 --> 00:05:05.600
Replatforming
business-critical workloads

00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:08.640
onto open source
databases can be daunting,

00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:11.750
especially when workloads
have strict scalability,

00:05:11.750 --> 00:05:15.550
performance, or
reliability requirements.

00:05:15.550 --> 00:05:17.800
Enterprise customers
moving from legacy,

00:05:17.800 --> 00:05:21.130
proprietary database
expected additional support

00:05:21.130 --> 00:05:24.740
in managing and scaling
their applications.

00:05:24.740 --> 00:05:27.220
And, finally, we think
there are opportunities

00:05:27.220 --> 00:05:30.010
to help customers do more
with their operational data,

00:05:30.010 --> 00:05:34.900
like real-time insights and the
integrated use of AI and ML.

00:05:34.900 --> 00:05:35.980
Don't get me wrong.

00:05:35.980 --> 00:05:38.480
We think Postgres is phenomenal.

00:05:38.480 --> 00:05:41.030
It's a phenomenally
mature database.

00:05:41.030 --> 00:05:44.222
It's just that customers
have asked us to help them

00:05:44.222 --> 00:05:45.430
solve some of these problems.

00:05:49.750 --> 00:05:53.080
We believed we at Google
were uniquely positioned

00:05:53.080 --> 00:05:55.330
to address these
user pain points.

00:05:55.330 --> 00:05:58.810
So we started on the journey
with Postgres, the world's most

00:05:58.810 --> 00:06:01.870
loved relational database,
and added the elements

00:06:01.870 --> 00:06:03.040
we know best--

00:06:03.040 --> 00:06:05.350
cloud-native
architectures, using

00:06:05.350 --> 00:06:08.140
Google's unique disaggregated
compute and storage

00:06:08.140 --> 00:06:10.780
architecture to scale
performance and improve

00:06:10.780 --> 00:06:14.170
availability, an
awesome engineering team

00:06:14.170 --> 00:06:17.770
assembled with database experts
from across the industry,

00:06:17.770 --> 00:06:21.580
and our DNA of AI and
ML to make everything

00:06:21.580 --> 00:06:25.260
about running a database easier.

00:06:25.260 --> 00:06:28.470
Alloys are made by melting
and fusing elements

00:06:28.470 --> 00:06:33.680
together to create something
new with improved properties.

00:06:33.680 --> 00:06:37.550
Today, we're excited to
announce the preview of AlloyDB

00:06:37.550 --> 00:06:38.990
for Postgres.

00:06:38.990 --> 00:06:42.290
Combining the best of
Google with full Postgres

00:06:42.290 --> 00:06:46.220
compatibility, AlloyDB
is a new database service

00:06:46.220 --> 00:06:50.430
that is ready for your top-tier
relational database workloads.

00:06:50.430 --> 00:06:53.270
AlloyDB provides
industry-leading performance

00:06:53.270 --> 00:06:55.220
and availability,
so it can handle

00:06:55.220 --> 00:06:58.710
any commercial-grade
workload you throw at it.

00:06:58.710 --> 00:07:00.960
Full Postgres
compatibility means

00:07:00.960 --> 00:07:04.290
regaining the freedom to
run your applications when

00:07:04.290 --> 00:07:06.630
and where you want
and benefiting

00:07:06.630 --> 00:07:11.340
from the entire
Postgres ecosystem.

00:07:11.340 --> 00:07:14.730
AlloyDB is four times faster
than standard Postgres

00:07:14.730 --> 00:07:16.800
for transactional workloads.

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:18.870
It's also more than
two times faster

00:07:18.870 --> 00:07:21.870
than Amazon's comparable
Postgres-compatible service

00:07:21.870 --> 00:07:24.000
for transactional workloads.

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:26.520
This means you can
scale transactions well

00:07:26.520 --> 00:07:29.970
beyond previous limitations
and get better performance out

00:07:29.970 --> 00:07:34.610
of fewer resources and much
smaller database instances.

00:07:34.610 --> 00:07:37.160
And best of all, query
performance scales

00:07:37.160 --> 00:07:40.280
linearly as you add
more compute resources.

00:07:43.490 --> 00:07:46.060
We also know that
customers use Postgres

00:07:46.060 --> 00:07:49.340
and other operational databases
for a variety of workloads,

00:07:49.340 --> 00:07:53.230
including real-time
insights and reporting.

00:07:53.230 --> 00:07:58.420
AlloyDB delivers up to 100
times faster analytical queries

00:07:58.420 --> 00:07:59.890
than standard Postgres.

00:07:59.890 --> 00:08:01.570
Isn't that amazing?

00:08:01.570 --> 00:08:04.420
That's with zero schema
changes and no need

00:08:04.420 --> 00:08:05.770
to move data around.

00:08:05.770 --> 00:08:09.160
We're really excited about
what this means for end users--

00:08:09.160 --> 00:08:13.540
super fast, real-time
analytical queries directly

00:08:13.540 --> 00:08:15.370
against your
operational database.

00:08:19.162 --> 00:08:22.590
At AlloyDB's core is
Google's innovative approach

00:08:22.590 --> 00:08:25.960
around completely disaggregating
compute and storage.

00:08:25.960 --> 00:08:28.140
This approach has
enabled us at Google

00:08:28.140 --> 00:08:31.380
to build truly unique and
differentiated systems, where

00:08:31.380 --> 00:08:34.080
we don't just separate
compute and storage at fixed

00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:37.650
layers of the stack, but we
have complete flexibility

00:08:37.650 --> 00:08:39.960
to add compute as
much or as little

00:08:39.960 --> 00:08:42.299
as needed within a
system, which enables

00:08:42.299 --> 00:08:45.720
us to deliver the most
performant, efficient,

00:08:45.720 --> 00:08:46.890
and scalable systems.

00:08:49.650 --> 00:08:52.770
For AlloyDB, we've built
a new intelligent database

00:08:52.770 --> 00:08:55.350
optimized storage layer
that scales separately

00:08:55.350 --> 00:08:59.550
from the database instances and
offloads work from the primary.

00:08:59.550 --> 00:09:01.590
But we're not the
first to do that.

00:09:01.590 --> 00:09:04.140
What's so special
about AlloyDB is

00:09:04.140 --> 00:09:07.050
that we're leveraging Google
technology to disaggregate

00:09:07.050 --> 00:09:09.790
compute and storage at
every layer of the stack,

00:09:09.790 --> 00:09:12.420
including the
storage layer itself.

00:09:12.420 --> 00:09:15.360
That allows us to deliver
a truly scalable system

00:09:15.360 --> 00:09:17.220
with predictable
high performance

00:09:17.220 --> 00:09:20.550
for both reads and writes,
and lots of flexibility

00:09:20.550 --> 00:09:23.630
to continue to innovate
in creative ways.

00:09:23.630 --> 00:09:26.390
Zooming in a little bit, you
can see the storage layer

00:09:26.390 --> 00:09:27.840
in more detail.

00:09:27.840 --> 00:09:30.980
It's powering fast,
predictable performance

00:09:30.980 --> 00:09:34.520
by eliminating I/O bottlenecks
and offloading work

00:09:34.520 --> 00:09:37.610
from the primary
database instance.

00:09:37.610 --> 00:09:40.640
And its regional by
default, enabling fast,

00:09:40.640 --> 00:09:45.440
bounded recovery times,
and low-lag read replicas.

00:09:45.440 --> 00:09:49.340
But again, what's so important
here is that, as you can see,

00:09:49.340 --> 00:09:52.280
the storage engine
itself has disaggregated

00:09:52.280 --> 00:09:53.970
compute and storage.

00:09:53.970 --> 00:09:56.990
This means that we can very
transparently rebalance load

00:09:56.990 --> 00:09:58.940
if there's a hotspot
and make sure

00:09:58.940 --> 00:10:02.720
that a particular shard gets
the compute resources it needs.

00:10:02.720 --> 00:10:05.930
It also allows us to scale
I/O because we can flexibly

00:10:05.930 --> 00:10:09.260
add more processors in the
intelligent storage engine

00:10:09.260 --> 00:10:11.120
to adapt to the load.

00:10:11.120 --> 00:10:14.030
Again, this is all fully
transparent to the database

00:10:14.030 --> 00:10:15.800
layer.

00:10:15.800 --> 00:10:18.550
The storage service isn't
the only technology powering

00:10:18.550 --> 00:10:21.160
fast, predictable performance.

00:10:21.160 --> 00:10:25.030
AlloyDB features automatic
data tiering between DRAM,

00:10:25.030 --> 00:10:28.765
and ultrafast cache,
and durable storage.

00:10:28.765 --> 00:10:30.640
That means you get the
availability and scale

00:10:30.640 --> 00:10:33.160
benefits of our scale-out
storage service,

00:10:33.160 --> 00:10:38.130
plus the price performance
benefits of tiered caches.

00:10:38.130 --> 00:10:41.850
We also intelligently organize
your data between those caches.

00:10:41.850 --> 00:10:45.780
In DRAM, we use both
row-based and columnar formats

00:10:45.780 --> 00:10:48.540
to maximize query performance.

00:10:48.540 --> 00:10:50.520
Machine learning
infers your workload

00:10:50.520 --> 00:10:54.180
and auto-populates these
caches effectively.

00:10:54.180 --> 00:10:57.510
We continuously monitor your
workload and dynamically

00:10:57.510 --> 00:10:59.670
decide which columns
and tables belong

00:10:59.670 --> 00:11:02.670
in the columnar store
then automatically convert

00:11:02.670 --> 00:11:05.760
them to columnar format.

00:11:05.760 --> 00:11:08.220
And so when a query comes
in, the query planner

00:11:08.220 --> 00:11:12.750
will smartly choose between
row-based, columnar, and hybrid

00:11:12.750 --> 00:11:16.770
execution to optimize
performance for every query.

00:11:16.770 --> 00:11:19.200
Within the columnar
store, AlloyDB

00:11:19.200 --> 00:11:20.850
builds on the
Single-Instruction,

00:11:20.850 --> 00:11:25.020
Multiple-Data, SIMD capability
of modern processors

00:11:25.020 --> 00:11:28.800
to speed up query execution.

00:11:28.800 --> 00:11:32.280
Cache misses go to a
second ultrafast tier cache

00:11:32.280 --> 00:11:36.570
before hitting durable storage
to maximize price-performance.

00:11:39.500 --> 00:11:41.510
This architecture
allows us to deliver

00:11:41.510 --> 00:11:43.910
the best performance
for both transactional

00:11:43.910 --> 00:11:45.830
and analytical workloads.

00:11:45.830 --> 00:11:48.590
The vectorized columnar
execution engine

00:11:48.590 --> 00:11:50.540
speeds up analytical queries.

00:11:50.540 --> 00:11:54.050
And intelligent storage
service powers very low-lag

00:11:54.050 --> 00:11:57.590
read replicas, so you're always
querying an up-to-date version

00:11:57.590 --> 00:12:00.090
of your data.

00:12:00.090 --> 00:12:02.060
That means you can
run super fast,

00:12:02.060 --> 00:12:05.570
real-time queries on your
operational data directly with

00:12:05.570 --> 00:12:08.600
zero impact on your
operational workload--

00:12:08.600 --> 00:12:11.600
no schema changes, no
additional indexes,

00:12:11.600 --> 00:12:13.800
no additional configuration.

00:12:13.800 --> 00:12:16.310
In fact, you might
even be able to drop

00:12:16.310 --> 00:12:19.430
your existing analytical
indexes to further improve

00:12:19.430 --> 00:12:21.640
transactional performance.

00:12:21.640 --> 00:12:27.800
AlloyDB delivers real-time
insights out of the box.

00:12:27.800 --> 00:12:32.630
To see that in action, I'm
going to turn it over to Viral.

00:12:32.630 --> 00:12:33.560
VIRAL SHAH: Hi.

00:12:33.560 --> 00:12:37.550
I'm Viral Shah, a Lead
Engineer for AlloyDB.

00:12:37.550 --> 00:12:40.010
As Andi mentioned,
AlloyDB allows

00:12:40.010 --> 00:12:44.510
you to supercharge your queries
with zero schema changes.

00:12:44.510 --> 00:12:46.820
Let's walk through a
call center use case

00:12:46.820 --> 00:12:51.950
to see how much faster
AlloyDB is than PostgreSQL.

00:12:51.950 --> 00:12:54.980
Let's say I'm managing a
call center CRM database.

00:12:54.980 --> 00:12:59.540
I have 1 terabyte of data
partitioned by day of month

00:12:59.540 --> 00:13:04.710
with approximately 100 GB on
the size for two months of data.

00:13:04.710 --> 00:13:07.540
The tables hold the
clients' interaction data,

00:13:07.540 --> 00:13:11.280
including the time they were
put on hold before the call was

00:13:11.280 --> 00:13:12.420
answered.

00:13:12.420 --> 00:13:16.050
My CRM database was initially
billed as an operational store

00:13:16.050 --> 00:13:20.110
to [INAUDIBLE] our call
centers, and continues to do so.

00:13:20.110 --> 00:13:23.490
But I'd like to get some
quick insights on our users'

00:13:23.490 --> 00:13:24.520
experience.

00:13:24.520 --> 00:13:26.460
So I'd like to
run a few queries.

00:13:26.460 --> 00:13:29.970
First, I'd like to understand
the average customer's hold

00:13:29.970 --> 00:13:33.990
time during the months of
July and August of 2021.

00:13:33.990 --> 00:13:36.300
As I'm building my
dashboard, we will

00:13:36.300 --> 00:13:38.820
be running three more
queries concurrently

00:13:38.820 --> 00:13:41.940
that are looking for the total
number of clients who called

00:13:41.940 --> 00:13:45.930
during those months, the client
that had maximum hold time,

00:13:45.930 --> 00:13:50.030
and the time of the
peak call volume.

00:13:50.030 --> 00:13:53.450
We have built a dashboard in
our favorite visualization

00:13:53.450 --> 00:13:56.990
tool, Looker, to compare
the performance of the two

00:13:56.990 --> 00:13:58.220
databases.

00:13:58.220 --> 00:14:01.250
On the left side, we
have an AlloyDB instance.

00:14:01.250 --> 00:14:05.910
And on the right side, we
have a PostgreSQL instance.

00:14:05.910 --> 00:14:07.520
The results of the
four queries are

00:14:07.520 --> 00:14:10.400
displayed below each of them.

00:14:10.400 --> 00:14:13.370
Before we dive in, let's
talk about the configuration.

00:14:13.370 --> 00:14:17.030
We have two like-for-like
VMs with 32 cores

00:14:17.030 --> 00:14:20.240
and 256 GB memory in each.

00:14:20.240 --> 00:14:23.210
For the AlloyDB VM, we
have provisioned a larger

00:14:23.210 --> 00:14:28.250
60-GB columnar cache alongside
a smaller 10-GB buffer cache,

00:14:28.250 --> 00:14:33.670
whereas the PostgreSQL VM has
a large 100-GB buffer cache.

00:14:33.670 --> 00:14:36.430
PostgreSQL's buffer cache
and AlloyDB's columnar cache

00:14:36.430 --> 00:14:38.440
have been prewarmed
and populated

00:14:38.440 --> 00:14:41.620
with the tables for
two months of data.

00:14:41.620 --> 00:14:43.420
I'm now going to
rerun the queries

00:14:43.420 --> 00:14:45.055
by refreshing the local cache.

00:14:49.210 --> 00:14:51.340
As you can see,
AlloyDB has already

00:14:51.340 --> 00:14:55.330
processed the query, whereas
PostgreSQL has some work to do.

00:14:55.330 --> 00:14:57.760
I think we'll have to
fast-forward this video

00:14:57.760 --> 00:15:00.610
to allow PostgreSQL's
to catch up.

00:15:00.610 --> 00:15:02.710
But how fast was AlloyDB?

00:15:02.710 --> 00:15:06.130
Let's look at this
dashboard to dive deeper.

00:15:06.130 --> 00:15:09.520
We see that the queries
running on PostgreSQL instance

00:15:09.520 --> 00:15:13.990
took an average of 164 seconds,
while running on an AlloyDB

00:15:13.990 --> 00:15:17.350
instance, they completed
in less than a second.

00:15:17.350 --> 00:15:21.910
AlloyDB was nearly 160 times
faster for these queries,

00:15:21.910 --> 00:15:26.470
and I didn't have to add any
indexes or change any schemas.

00:15:26.470 --> 00:15:28.112
Cool.

00:15:28.112 --> 00:15:29.570
ANDI GUTMANS: That
was a cool demo.

00:15:29.570 --> 00:15:31.970
Thank you so much.

00:15:31.970 --> 00:15:34.570
We're so excited about the
performance of that AlloyDB

00:15:34.570 --> 00:15:37.720
offers customers and the new
capabilities and insights

00:15:37.720 --> 00:15:38.950
it unlocks.

00:15:38.950 --> 00:15:40.750
But this architecture
also enables

00:15:40.750 --> 00:15:43.780
a new level of reliability
and availability.

00:15:43.780 --> 00:15:46.810
We offer four-nines
availability SLA

00:15:46.810 --> 00:15:49.090
that not only includes
unplanned failures,

00:15:49.090 --> 00:15:51.260
but database
maintenance as well,

00:15:51.260 --> 00:15:54.120
so you can better
serve your end users.

00:15:54.120 --> 00:15:57.070
AlloyDB automatically
detects and recovers

00:15:57.070 --> 00:16:01.210
from most database failures
within 60 seconds, independent

00:16:01.210 --> 00:16:02.800
of database size and load.

00:16:02.800 --> 00:16:05.150
It means recovery
is not only fast,

00:16:05.150 --> 00:16:07.530
but also very predictable.

00:16:07.530 --> 00:16:11.220
Our architecture also supports
online instance resizing

00:16:11.220 --> 00:16:13.320
and database maintenance.

00:16:13.320 --> 00:16:16.830
The primary instance can resume
normal operations in seconds,

00:16:16.830 --> 00:16:22.330
while replica pull updates are
fully transparent to users.

00:16:22.330 --> 00:16:24.900
And finally, we'll talk
about this more in a bit

00:16:24.900 --> 00:16:27.600
when I cover how easy
AlloyDB is to manage--

00:16:27.600 --> 00:16:29.700
we've done a lot of
work to make sure

00:16:29.700 --> 00:16:31.980
that other reliability
issues you see,

00:16:31.980 --> 00:16:35.100
like out-of-memory errors
and out-of-disk errors,

00:16:35.100 --> 00:16:36.255
are a thing of the past.

00:16:39.530 --> 00:16:42.260
Writes on AlloyDB scale
linearly with core

00:16:42.260 --> 00:16:45.530
count up to the largest instance
supported on Google Compute

00:16:45.530 --> 00:16:47.030
Engine.

00:16:47.030 --> 00:16:48.980
Reads scale
horizontally, with loads

00:16:48.980 --> 00:16:52.280
balancing across more
than a thousand vCPUs.

00:16:52.280 --> 00:16:55.820
Each read replica has its
own vectorized columnar store

00:16:55.820 --> 00:16:58.910
that's auto-populated by
access pattern recognized

00:16:58.910 --> 00:17:03.300
by the machine learning
algorithms on that replica.

00:17:03.300 --> 00:17:05.369
And, of course, storage
scales automatically

00:17:05.369 --> 00:17:09.119
as well due to that scale-out
intelligence storage engine

00:17:09.119 --> 00:17:10.079
I described earlier.

00:17:12.880 --> 00:17:16.839
Talip is going to show us how
seamlessly reconnections scale

00:17:16.839 --> 00:17:19.004
with AlloyDB.

00:17:19.004 --> 00:17:20.150
TALIP OZTURK: Hi.

00:17:20.150 --> 00:17:22.130
My name is Talip Ozturk.

00:17:22.130 --> 00:17:26.480
I am the Engineering Manager
for AlloyDB read pools.

00:17:26.480 --> 00:17:29.840
Read pools allow you
to horizontally scale

00:17:29.840 --> 00:17:32.840
read connections by
adding more nodes.

00:17:32.840 --> 00:17:35.720
Today, I want to
show you how you

00:17:35.720 --> 00:17:37.880
can increase your
read performance

00:17:37.880 --> 00:17:41.040
by adding more nodes.

00:17:41.040 --> 00:17:43.870
Here, I have a cluster
with a primary and a read

00:17:43.870 --> 00:17:45.090
pool of two nodes.

00:17:45.090 --> 00:17:47.530
I'm expecting an
increase in traffic,

00:17:47.530 --> 00:17:50.860
so I want to double the
read capacity on my cluster.

00:17:50.860 --> 00:17:52.140
It's very simple.

00:17:52.140 --> 00:17:57.820
Just edit the pool and change
the node count from 2 to 4.

00:17:57.820 --> 00:18:00.160
Because this update
takes a few minutes,

00:18:00.160 --> 00:18:04.180
I created another cluster
with multiple pools.

00:18:04.180 --> 00:18:07.900
As you can see, the first
pool only has one node,

00:18:07.900 --> 00:18:12.460
the second has two nodes,
and the third has four nodes.

00:18:12.460 --> 00:18:15.820
They are all backed by the same
primary and the same database

00:18:15.820 --> 00:18:16.850
storage.

00:18:16.850 --> 00:18:20.440
That means, regardless of
the endpoint I connect to,

00:18:20.440 --> 00:18:23.260
I will be querying the
exact same database.

00:18:23.260 --> 00:18:25.420
Because node counts
are different,

00:18:25.420 --> 00:18:27.550
I expect that my
query per second

00:18:27.550 --> 00:18:31.480
should double from one node
to two nodes and two nodes

00:18:31.480 --> 00:18:32.560
to four nodes.

00:18:32.560 --> 00:18:35.080
Let's see if that's the case.

00:18:35.080 --> 00:18:37.090
Here, I have a
client VM, and I will

00:18:37.090 --> 00:18:40.300
be running a standard
pgbench select-only workload

00:18:40.300 --> 00:18:41.570
for 10 seconds.

00:18:41.570 --> 00:18:45.340
Let's run it against the
first pool with one node.

00:18:45.340 --> 00:18:46.975
Its IP ends with 28.

00:18:51.650 --> 00:18:56.160
It is 71,000
transactions per second.

00:18:56.160 --> 00:18:59.490
Let's try the pool
with two nodes.

00:18:59.490 --> 00:19:02.070
Its IP ends with 15.

00:19:02.070 --> 00:19:05.490
Just going to change IP,
and that should double,

00:19:05.490 --> 00:19:12.040
and it should be more
than 140,000 per second.

00:19:12.040 --> 00:19:16.770
Great, it's 149,000
transactions per second.

00:19:16.770 --> 00:19:20.060
Now, let's see if we
can double it again.

00:19:20.060 --> 00:19:24.020
This time, we're going to be
connecting to the last pool,

00:19:24.020 --> 00:19:26.960
and its IP ends with 19.

00:19:26.960 --> 00:19:30.970
That should be around 300,000.

00:19:30.970 --> 00:19:35.470
Awesome, 300,000
transactions per second.

00:19:35.470 --> 00:19:38.560
As you can see, we can literally
scale our read performance

00:19:38.560 --> 00:19:39.790
by adding more nodes.

00:19:39.790 --> 00:19:44.110
I can scale up and down in
just a few minutes at any time.

00:19:44.110 --> 00:19:47.410
It's super easy to make sure
you have the right capacity

00:19:47.410 --> 00:19:48.620
for your workload.

00:19:48.620 --> 00:19:52.330
I want to show you one more
thing before we end our demo.

00:19:52.330 --> 00:19:55.390
As I said before,
all these read pools

00:19:55.390 --> 00:19:57.070
are backed by the same primary.

00:19:57.070 --> 00:19:59.260
So what happens if
the primary dies?

00:19:59.260 --> 00:20:00.940
Can we still use the read pools?

00:20:00.940 --> 00:20:02.110
Let's see.

00:20:02.110 --> 00:20:04.900
First, let's see if we can
connect to the primary.

00:20:04.900 --> 00:20:07.090
I'm just going to use psql.

00:20:07.090 --> 00:20:11.680
Primary IP with 2, so I'm
just going to connect to it.

00:20:11.680 --> 00:20:13.720
Great, we can.

00:20:13.720 --> 00:20:17.740
Now, I'm going to crash the
primary and also its standby

00:20:17.740 --> 00:20:20.090
with this command.

00:20:20.090 --> 00:20:24.180
Now let's see if he
can access the primary.

00:20:24.180 --> 00:20:26.940
Primary is definitely down.

00:20:26.940 --> 00:20:28.690
Let's see if we can
access the read pools.

00:20:32.490 --> 00:20:34.200
Great.

00:20:34.200 --> 00:20:36.600
Even if the primary
is down, read pools

00:20:36.600 --> 00:20:39.000
can continue running just fine.

00:20:39.000 --> 00:20:41.880
Hope you enjoyed the demo
and thank you for your time.

00:20:41.880 --> 00:20:44.000
Back to you, Andi.

00:20:44.000 --> 00:20:45.720
ANDI GUTMANS: That
was an awesome demo.

00:20:45.720 --> 00:20:47.950
Thank you.

00:20:47.950 --> 00:20:50.320
AlloyDB is also
really easy to manage.

00:20:50.320 --> 00:20:53.140
We use machine learning and
other adaptive algorithms

00:20:53.140 --> 00:20:54.940
to provide
comprehensive database

00:20:54.940 --> 00:20:58.870
